P}}_{n,j}(r)}{{\\tilde{P}}_{n,0}(r)}.\n\n## Advances in Mathematics\n\nDate: 22 January 2022\n\nArticle: 108028\n\nVolume: Volume 394\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\nResearch article\n\nOpen archive\n\nUnder an Elsevier user license\n\nGet rights and content\n\n# An analytic proof of the Borwein Conjecture☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Chen Wang\n\nShow more\n\nView PDFDownload full issue\n\n10.1016/j.aim.2021.108028\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe provide a proof of the Borwein Conjecture, which states that the coefficients of have a repeating sign pattern of , using analytic methods. The proof is done by utilizing an expansion by Andrews to extract the “main part” of the coefficients, and then bound the various “error terms” that arise from this expansion.\n\n## Keywords\n\nBorwein Conjecture\n\n;\n\nq-series\n\n;\n\nSaddle point method\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nIn 1990, Peter Borwein observed that for an arbitrary non-negative integer n, the coefficients of the polynomial have a repeating sign pattern of . A more formalized version appears in a 1995 paper by Andrews [1]. Here, and in the sequel, we use the standard notation for q-shifted factorials,\n\nConjecture 1.1\n\n## P. Borwein\n\nLet the polynomials , and be defined by the relationship(1.1) Then these polynomials have non-negative coefficients.\n\nThis statement is known as the Borwein Conjecture.\n\nThere have been many attempts to prove the Borwein Conjecture. Moreover, we find several variations and generalizations in the literature, see [1], [5], [6], [8], [18], [19], [21], sometimes also conjecturally, sometimes with full or partial proofs. However, none of the proved variations and generalizations cover the original conjecture, Conjecture 1.1. It is fair to say that so far essentially two methods have been tried: bijective methods—such as in [8], [13], and basic hypergeometric methods—such as in [1], [5], [19]. \nConjecture 14.1\n\n## P. Borwein\n\nLet the polynomials , and be defined by the relationship(14.1) Then these polynomials have non-negative coefficients.\n\nConjecture 14.2\n\n## P. Borwein\n\nLet the polynomials , , , and be defined by the relationship(14.2) Then these polynomials have non-negative coefficients.\n\nBoth these conjectures are still wide open. In particular, no reasonable formulas for the polynomials have been found so far. We remark that the comparison of (1.1) and (14.1) yields the relationship , so non-negativity for the coefficients of follows trivially from this paper.\n\nRecall that for our proof we used the formulas for , and given in Theorem 1.2. As we mentioned, these formulas had apparently not caught much attention so far. It is rather a different type of formula that was found to be much more inspiring, namely (see [1, Theorem 3.1])(14.3){A}_{n}(q)=\\sum _{j\\in \\mathbb{Z}}{(-1)}^{j}{q}^{j(9j+1)/2}{\\left[\\begin{array}{c}2n\\\\ n+3j\\end{array}\\right]}_{q},(14.4){B}_{n}(q)=\\sum _{j\\in \\mathbb{Z}}{(-1)}^{j}{q}^{j(9j-5)/2}{\\left[\\begin{array}{c}2n\\\\ n+3j-1\\end{array}\\right]}_{q},(14.5){C}_{n}(q)=\\sum _{j\\in \\mathbb{Z}}{(-1)}^{j}{q}^{j(9j+7)/2}{\\left[\\begin{array}{c}2n\\\\ n+3j+1\\end{array}\\right]}_{q}, where we used again the standard notation for q-binomial coefficients. These are so much more imaginative because of their resemblance with a family of formulas appearing as generating functions for partitions with restricted hook differences in [2]. Andrews et al. had shown that(14.6) is the generating function for certain partitions with restricted hook differences, with being non-negative integers satisfying and . Indeed, the generating function in (14.3) is the “special case” of (14.6) in which , , and . Similar observations hold for and . In other words, the result of Andrews et al. seems to produce a proof of the Borwein Conjecture, except for the small flaw that the choices of α and β are not integral, and thus not legitimate.\n\nBressoud [8] extended the mystery by making the following much more general conj